{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018xzx6fHG5MhVQX4NZmhjVq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Counsel and the conversation: rulings of 2026-09-25\n2\t\n3\tObserved on #219 (R3's own build): attempt 1 halted after 3,510 s as `non-question`; `counsel-01` read it, verdict `answer`, text \"Continue the build from the existing working tree\"; under R2 the product filed an issue and waited for the operator. Under R3 counsel's `answer` is the next attempt's steer.\n4\t\n5\t## Troubleshoot, measured\n6\t\n7\tOne `attavo counsel 219 --ask \"why did this build stop?\"` in a scratch checkout: 73 s, three model turns. Turn one answered with no citations; the adapter's citation guard sent it back; it read the record, the last 30 KB of the 4.8 MB build log, searched for the brief and found nothing (the corpus was not in the repository); turn three answered with citations. It wrote `counsel-02.json` into the scratch checkout, which was discarded.\n8\t\n9\t## Ruled\n10\t\n11\t1. A question to counsel is a follow-up to a stop counsel has already read. `attavo counsel N --ask` is handed the prior counsel records for the item as its first input (verdict, text, citations), never re-derives a class a record states, and writes its answer as the next `counsel-NN.json` with `answers` naming the counsel record it follows beside `record`. The screen commits and pushes it like any counsel record. The answer is a record: an intervention in the attestation and precedent.\n12\t2. The panel's first message is the newest counsel record read from the branch (no invocation); `--ask` runs only for a question the operator types.\n13\t3. Resuming a vendor's own thread is an adapter-internal optimisation, recorded in the adapter's report when used, never part of the contract; the records are the mechanism that carries context between invocations.\n14\t4. For a level that is the operator's (budget spent, two identical failures, counsel says `person`) the panel opens with the exhaustion report and ends in one of three drafted acts, each a record: a ruling (R4), a spec amendment (moves the approval), a product or tenant defect (counsel's `patch`).\n15\t5. Counsel is handed the stop record, the signature and the stage's last events rather than the whole log, unless the question is about the log.\n16\t6. Counsel's corpus, the text it reads before it answers (method rules, briefs, rulings), lives in the repository under `docs/`, named by `[counsel].reads`; a manifest naming a path that does not exist is refused.\n17\t\n18\t## The conversation and its records\n19\t\n20\tThe exchange is split from its outcome. A turn in the conversation is working material and lives in the product's datastore with the item; a record is written only for an outcome the attestation cares about: counsel's verdict on a stop (answer, patch, person); a follow-up whose answer changes what the next attempt is told; or an operator turning a turn into an act (a ruling, a spec amendment, an issue). The record carries the question that produced it. A question that belongs to Plan is answered and routed there; in Execute the only outcomes are those above, so a tangent produces nothing in git however long it runs.\n21\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4ee75ad-edac-4244-be61-f864406c706c","uuid":"01f5d3fc-e9ff-4f53-b2cf-ebcfdf65b89d","timestamp":"2026-10-01T19:48:53.301Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-277/attest/specs/277/stub/docs/counsel-rulings.md","content":"# Counsel and the conversation: rulings of 2026-09-25\n\nObserved on #219 (R3's own build): attempt 1 halted after 3,510 s as `non-question`; `counsel-01` read it, verdict `answer`, text \"Continue the build from the existing working tree\"; under R2 the product filed an issue and waited for the operator. Under R3 counsel's `answer` is the next attempt's steer.\n\n## Troubleshoot, measured\n\nOne `attavo counsel 219 --ask \"why did this build stop?\"` in a scratch checkout: 73 s, three model turns. Turn one answered with no citations; the adapter's citation guard sent it back; it read the record, the last 30 KB of the 4.8 MB build log, searched for the brief and found nothing (the corpus was not in the repository); turn three answered with citations. It wrote `counsel-02.json` into the scratch checkout, which was discarded.\n\n## Ruled\n\n1. A question to counsel is a follow-up to a stop counsel has already read. `attavo counsel N --ask` is handed the prior counsel records for the item as its first input (verdict, text, citations), never re-derives a class a record states, and writes its answer as the next `counsel-NN.json` with `answers` naming the counsel record it follows beside `record`. The screen commits and pushes it like any counsel record. The answer is a record: an intervention in the attestation and precedent.\n2. The panel's first message is the newest counsel record read from the branch (no invocation); `--ask` runs only for a question the operator types.\n3. Resuming a vendor's own thread is an adapter-internal optimisation, recorded in the adapter's report when used, never part of the contract; the records are the mechanism that carries context between invocations.\n4. For a level that is the operator's (budget spent, two identical failures, counsel says `person`) the panel opens with the exhaustion report and ends in one of three drafted acts, each a record: a ruling (R4), a spec amendment (moves the approval), a product or tenant defect (counsel's `patch`).\n5. Counsel is handed the stop record, the signature and the stage's last events rather than the whole log, unless the question is about the log.\n6. Counsel's corpus, the text it reads before it answers (method rules, briefs, rulings), lives in the repository under `docs/`, named by `[counsel].reads`; a manifest naming a path that does not exist is refused.\n\n## The conversation and its records\n\nThe exchange is split from its outcome. A turn in the conversation is working material and lives in the product's datastore with the item; a record is written only for an outcome the attestation cares about: counsel's verdict on a stop (answer, patch, person); a follow-up whose answer changes what the next attempt is told; or an operator turning a turn into an act (a ruling, a spec amendment, an issue). The record carries the question that produced it. A question that belongs to Plan is answered and routed there; in Execute the only outcomes are those above, so a tangent produces nothing in git however long it runs.\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cfc6YS276SbWTxBrgAvbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NHVbdzcr8oC51VtBCfnmLy","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-277/attest/specs/277/stub/docs/r3-rulings.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1171,"cache_read_input_tokens":72155,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1171},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4ee75ad-edac-4244-be61-f864406c706c","uuid":"49ab62d5-f010-498b-b771-debf07ac857a","timestamp":"2026-10-01T19:48:55.298Z","request_id":"req_011Cfc6YRf2UyusSqAjBRzyw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NHVbdzcr8oC51VtBCfnmLy":{"file_path":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-277/attest/specs/277/stub/docs/r3-rulings.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NHVbdzcr8oC51VtBCfnmLy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# R3 (retries, bounds and budgets): the nine rulings of 2026-09-25\n2\t\n3\tAll nine proposed by the session and ruled, agreed, by the operator on 2026-09-25 with the session's recommendations adopted. They sit on top of ruling C and its two amendments in the reconciler brief (2026-09-20). Item #219 carries the spec; v0.28.0 carries the code.\n4\t\n5\t1. **Failure signature.** `stop.signature` on every non-pass stop, compared by string equality, runs of digits removed on every family: last event line (died, hung); adapter's stop detail (transient, others); sorted names of red required checks (returned check); sorted titles of blocking findings (returned review). The exhaustion report prints signatures side by side; a signature too fine is the known failure mode and the side-by-side is how it is seen.\n6\t2. **What a returned retry carries.** The red check's log via `[forge].check_log_command`, or the review's blocking findings. Collapsed with 3 into one input.\n7\t3. **How a steer reaches the next attempt.** The existing `ATTAVO_FEEDBACK` file, seeded on the retried attempt's first iteration with class and signature, the evidence, and the newest steer newer than the blocking record (counsel's `answer` now, an operator's `/rule` after R4). The record carries `retry_of` and `steer`. No new adapter input.\n8\t4. **Transient wait.** `[drive].retry_after_s`, default 300; died and hung retry on the next pass with no wait.\n9\t5. **Ceilings.** `[drive.build].ceiling_s` and `[drive.verify].ceiling_s`, read against the corpus of build wall clocks at each pin; past it, terminated and recorded hung with `stop.bound = \"ceiling\"`; non-streaming adapters (`conformance.json` `streams_events: false`) are judged by the ceiling alone; the doctor refuses a `[drive]` table without both ceilings.\n10\t6. **Budget accounting.** Every marker counts, recovered attempts included; the report always says attempt N of the budget.\n11\t7. **`--again`.** Resets a spent budget only, recorded in the marker; a no-op inside the budget; retired in R4.\n12\t8. **Environment refusal.** Recorded in R3, never retried per item; acted on by R4's tenant-wide breaker.\n13\t9. **Exhaustion report's home.** `attavo drive N --report` (state stopped, `attempts`, `signatures_match`, `rulings_available`); no new command.\n14\t\n15\tFound while writing the spec: a review with findings passed whatever their severity (severity is free text); the returned-review family needed a definition, so a finding of severity `blocking` fails the review as `returned` and every other severity is filed as before. R2 as merged reads alive from the heartbeat and never calls a heartbeated worker hung by event age; the ceiling is therefore the only wall-clock bound a worker has, which is what ruling 5 intended.\n16\t\n17\tExclusions: no cross-executor retry; no backoff beyond the one fixed wait.\n18\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4ee75ad-edac-4244-be61-f864406c706c","uuid":"52b38416-1af3-4e0a-a874-fb6110ca9259","timestamp":"2026-10-01T19:48:55.304Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-277/attest/specs/277/stub/docs/r3-rulings.md","content":"# R3 (retries, bounds and budgets): the nine rulings of 2026-09-25\n\nAll nine proposed by the session and ruled, agreed, by the operator on 2026-09-25 with the session's recommendations adopted. They sit on top of ruling C and its two amendments in the reconciler brief (2026-09-20). Item #219 carries the spec; v0.28.0 carries the code.\n\n1. **Failure signature.** `stop.signature` on every non-pass stop, compared by string equality, runs of digits removed on every family: last event line (died, hung); adapter's stop detail (transient, others); sorted names of red required checks (returned check); sorted titles of blocking findings (returned review). The exhaustion report prints signatures side by side; a signature too fine is the known failure mode and the side-by-side is how it is seen.\n2. **What a returned retry carries.** The red check's log via `[forge].check_log_command`, or the review's blocking findings. Collapsed with 3 into one input.\n3. **How a steer reaches the next attempt.** The existing `ATTAVO_FEEDBACK` file, seeded on the retried attempt's first iteration with class and signature, the evidence, and the newest steer newer than the blocking record (counsel's `answer` now, an operator's `/rule` after R4). The record carries `retry_of` and `steer`. No new adapter input.\n4. **Transient wait.** `[drive].retry_after_s`, default 300; died and hung retry on the next pass with no wait.\n5. **Ceilings.** `[drive.build].ceiling_s` and `[drive.verify].ceiling_s`, read against the corpus of build wall clocks at each pin; past it, terminated and recorded hung with `stop.bound = \"ceiling\"`; non-streaming adapters (`conformance.json` `streams_events: false`) are judged by the ceiling alone; the doctor refuses a `[drive]` table without both ceilings.\n6. **Budget accounting.** Every marker counts, recovered attempts included; the report always says attempt N of the budget.\n7. **`--again`.** Resets a spent budget only, recorded in the marker; a no-op inside the budget; retired in R4.\n8. **Environment refusal.** Recorded in R3, never retried per item; acted on by R4's tenant-wide breaker.\n9. **Exhaustion report's home.** `attavo drive N --report` (state stopped, `attempts`, `signatures_match`, `rulings_available`); no new command.\n\nFound while writing the spec: a review with findings passed whatever their severity (severity is free text); the returned-review family needed a definition, so a finding of severity `blocking` fails the review as `returned` and every other severity is filed as before. R2 as merged reads alive from the heartbeat and never calls a heartbeated worker hung by event age; the ceiling is therefore the only wall-clock bound a worker has, which is what ruling 5 intended.\n\nExclusions: no cross-executor retry; no backoff beyond the one fixed wait.\n","numLines":18,"startLine":1,"totalLines":18}}}
